# Retroverse Design Language

Retroverse is NOT a modern SaaS app.

Retroverse should feel:
- tactile
- playful
- illustrated
- colorful
- editorial
- collectible
- poster-like
- retro-futurist
- hand-crafted

NOT:
- minimal SaaS
- dashboard UI
- glassmorphism
- sterile Apple-style minimalism
- fintech UI
- enterprise software
- monochrome interfaces

# Core Visual Rules

Always prefer:
- cream/paper backgrounds
- teal accents
- orange/red highlight accents
- warm retro color palettes
- thick outlines
- bold section framing
- oversized artwork
- playful layouts
- layered poster composition
- tactile card designs
- illustrated UI feeling
- large readable typography
- visible section separation
- collectible-card layouts
- magazine/editorial hierarchy

Avoid:
- gray-on-gray UI
- dark glass cards
- thin line minimalism
- floating SaaS cards
- giant rounded rectangles everywhere
- excessive blur
- generic Tailwind dashboards
- tiny metadata text
- overuse of gradients
- empty whitespace-heavy layouts

# Typography

Prefer:
- large bold headers
- friendly readable text
- strong hierarchy
- editorial composition

Avoid:
- tiny captions
- weak hierarchy
- overly compressed spacing

# Interaction Design

Retroverse should feel:
- exploratory
- tactile
- alive
- collectible
- emotional

Search should feel:
- immediate
- rich
- connected
- discovery-oriented

NOT:
- database querying
- admin tooling
- enterprise filtering

# Component Style

Cards should:
- maintain fixed geometry
- feel physical
- have strong visual edges
- use clear sectioning
- support oversized artwork

Panels should:
- feel like collectible shelves
- scroll horizontally
- suggest abundance and discovery

# Mobile-First

Always optimize for:
- mobile browsing
- thumb interaction
- oversized touch targets
- visual clarity on phones

# Frontend Behavior

Prefer:
- stable layouts
- smooth interactions
- immediate feedback
- lightweight transitions

Avoid:
- layout jumping
- over-animation
- slow loading interactions

# Architectural Guidance

Prefer:
- small focused components
- stable visual systems
- iterative enhancement

Avoid:
- giant rewrites
- unnecessary abstractions
- premature optimization
